# The `p`-central series meets the lower central series in `γ_j^{p^{i-j}} γ_{j+1} F_{i+1}`

This file proves the lemma `pSeries_inter_centralSeries` needed for the Shafarevich project: for a
free group `F = FreeGroup X` on a finite set, a prime `p` and `i ≥ j ≥ 1`,

`F_i ∩ γ_j ≤ ⟨γ_j ^{p^{i-j}}⟩ · γ_{j+1} · F_{i+1}`

in the paper's notation (`F_i` the descending `p`-central series, `γ_*` the descending central
series).  This is the ("weak", i.e. modulo the *next* `p`-central term) form of Lemma 4(ii) of

* A. Schmidt, K. Wingberg, *Šafarevič's theorem on solvable groups as Galois groups*,
  arXiv:math/9809211, §2,

which is exactly what the surjectivity part of Proposition 5 of that paper consumes: it says that
the graded piece `F_{(i,j)}/F_{(i,j)+1}` is generated by `p^{i-j}`-st powers of elements of `γ_j`.

## Strategy (and what is used)

The main argument of this file is an **induction on `i`** for the statement

`St i j : F_i ∩ γ_j ≤ ⟨γ_j ^{p^{i-j}}⟩ · γ_{j+1}`   (`j ≤ i`),

which is *stronger* than the required conclusion, because `γ_{j+1} ≤ F_{j+1} ≤ F_{i+1}` whenever
`j ≤ i`.  The induction step is the "collection step"

`gamma_pow_inter_pSeries_sup : γ_j ^{p^m} ∩ (F_{j+m+1} γ_{j+1}) ≤ γ_j ^{p^{m+1}} γ_{j+1}`,

proved in this file (`gamma_pow_inter_pSeries_sup` below) from the inclusion half of **Lemma 4(ii)**
of Schmidt–Wingberg, i.e. from the equality

`F_{j+m+1} ∩ γ_j = ⋁_{j ≤ k ≤ j+m+1} ⟨γ_k ^{p^{j+m+1-k}}⟩`

(`lemma4ii_filtration`, proved by the Magnus/Witt machinery for free groups in
`Submission/Work/SSGRoute/prop5/lemma4ii_filtration.lean`).  That equality is the genuinely
free-group-theoretic input (Witt's theorem that the lower central factors of a free group are
torsion free, together with the Magnus embedding); everything else here is the elementary
group-theoretic bookkeeping around it.

Concretely, if `x ∈ F_{i+1} ∩ γ_j` and (by induction) `x = a · b` with `a ∈ ⟨γ_j^{p^{i-j}}⟩` and
`b ∈ γ_{j+1}`, then `a = x b⁻¹ ∈ F_{i+1} γ_{j+1}`, and the collection step (with `m = i - j`)
upgrades `a` to a `p^{i+1-j}`-st power modulo `γ_{j+1}`; hence `x ∈ ⟨γ_j ^{p^{i+1-j}}⟩ γ_{j+1}`.
-/

import Mathlib
import Submission.Work.Shrinking.PCentral
import Submission.Work.Shrinking.pCentralSeries_antitone
import Submission.Work.SSGRoute.prop5.lemma4ii_filtration
